the eastern mediterranean is sweltering  under the most intense heatwave  to hit europe so far this year  temperatures across a huge region  from sicily to the balkans and turkey  are already well into the 40s  and the european record of 48 degrees  set in greece  44 years ago could be broken sometime  this week  so what's going on well a high pressure  system that originated in north africa  has been parked over the eastern  mediterranean for several days  it's now created a heat dome with warm  air  trapped by the high pressure above it  all that pressure squashes the air  heating it up even further and with no  clouds and sun-baked earth  it gets hotter by the day it was that  same phenomenon that caused the extreme  heat on the west coast of the us  and canada a few weeks ago climate  change is causing the jet stream in the  northern hemisphere to meander more  as it moves around the world leading to  bigger temperature variations  we've turned the thermostat up so when  these events happen  effectively they're going to happen more  frequently the duration  has more than doubled in some parts of  the world so they last a lot longer  and when they do happen they're much  more intense so we see temperature  records being broken on a much more  frequent basis  the heat and lack of rain are ideal  conditions for  wildfires in the last week huge numbers  have ignited across the region  turkey has so far lost almost 150  000 hectares of forest in 2021 more than  three times the area  would normally lose in a whole year  italy has lost  twice as much as it would normally do  each year so far the wildfires in greece  haven't been any worse than normal but  with  no rain in the forest until the second  half of august at least  that could change
 

